#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107150 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(4)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1] + [4^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 4 + 9 + 16; = 29\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8dc0c69-e0be-5d9c-bb2a-4838709fb9eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 733 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107151 :tutorial/content "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a right Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/6a/6aa3fd6dca84719aa7d34fdf75586a33005dd34c416d4456662f9d1876679f61.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+f(x_3)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_n) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09dcf1d5-eddf-58d0-8f4b-eeae9cf26024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 735 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Right Riemann Sum"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107152 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5}) + {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\\\ &= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5} \\\\ &=1+6+12 \\\\ &= 19. \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b662a09b-80ce-5d1d-8950-1d407892ead3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 736 :tutorial/title "Right Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107100 :tutorial/content "Whether the right Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](images/71/71bc3147a2d7ee5867740e67f55d2a257065f0f225b62b4af3b5f5990c0d4ffc.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/0b/0bc5bc2d5daa2b42e640d31b998707af503b4c4489531ef7ddd41f4374405ef7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca191bef-f207-5cb0-88a1-6c46288c5c09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4373 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"}]}
